WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
How can rain falling on a farm help build a crab in the Chesapeake Bay?

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.740
Keep your eyes on one watershed, where land, river, and Bay stay connected.

3
00:00:13.283 --> 00:00:18.043
Blue will track water, orange will track carbon, and green will track nitrogen.

4
00:00:19.403 --> 00:00:21.903
Each colored token can pause in a reservoir.

5
00:00:23.303 --> 00:00:28.623
A reservoir is a place where matter can stay, such as air, water, soil, or a living

6
00:00:28.623 --> 00:00:29.623
thing.

7
00:00:29.967 --> 00:00:33.167
Start with orange carbon dioxide in air and water.

8
00:00:34.627 --> 00:00:39.927
Algae and plants use photosynthesis to build sugars from carbon dioxide and water.

9
00:00:41.227 --> 00:00:45.287
Food moves that carbon through living things, until some reaches the crab.

10
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:51.110
The crab's cells use respiration, releasing carbon dioxide to the water.

11
00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:54.170
Plants and microbes respire too.

12
00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:58.810
When organisms die, decomposers break down their bodies.

13
00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:04.550
Microbial respiration returns much of their carbon to air or water.

14
00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:11.330
Water has its own route through evaporation, clouds, precipitation, and flow across

15
00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:14.490
land. It also carries other matter.

16
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:20.070
Blue runoff can pick up green nitrogen from a field and move it into a river.

17
00:01:21.183 --> 00:01:24.683
Most organisms cannot use nitrogen gas directly.

18
00:01:26.103 --> 00:01:30.563
Certain microbes convert it into ammonia, and other microbes help make nitrate.

19
00:01:31.983 --> 00:01:34.523
Plants and algae take up ammonium or nitrate.

20
00:01:35.823 --> 00:01:38.063
Food can then carry that nitrogen to the crab.

21
00:01:39.117 --> 00:01:41.977
Waste and dead bodies keep nitrogen moving.

22
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:46.257
Decomposers return their nitrogen to ammonium.

23
00:01:47.477 --> 00:01:52.617
In wet soil or mud with little oxygen, bacteria can change nitrate back into

24
00:01:52.617 --> 00:01:56.277
nitrogen gas. That gas returns to the air.

25
00:01:57.267 --> 00:02:00.327
Now follow an orange carbon token inside the crab.

26
00:02:01.587 --> 00:02:05.367
When the crab respires, which reservoir receives that carbon next?

27
00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:08.547
Hold your answer while the token waits.

28
00:02:14.583 --> 00:02:19.343
The surrounding water receives carbon dioxide from the crab's respiration.

29
00:02:20.563 --> 00:02:24.863
That carbon may later enter algae, leave the water, or travel elsewhere.

30
00:02:26.223 --> 00:02:30.783
A cycle is a network of possible routes, not one track every atom repeats.

31
00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:34.317
Notice how the paths share the same world.

32
00:02:35.657 --> 00:02:37.437
Blue water carries green nitrogen.

33
00:02:38.937 --> 00:02:41.937
Algae can hold orange carbon and green nitrogen together.

34
00:02:43.337 --> 00:02:47.817
Animals receive both through food, while decomposers send both onward again.

35
00:02:48.867 --> 00:02:53.487
At the Bay's surface, orange carbon dioxide can dissolve into seawater.

36
00:02:54.847 --> 00:02:57.107
Carbon dioxide also comes back out.

37
00:02:58.387 --> 00:03:03.427
Ocean absorption names one direction in a two-way exchange between air and water.

38
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:10.240
In ecosystems, atoms move between reservoirs, and processes connect their cycles.

39
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:13.020
The crab is not an endpoint.

40
00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:19.980
It is one temporary reservoir in a moving watershed of air, land, water, microbes,

41
00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:20.620
and food.
